!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math
!tags: Math Calculus Integration

!id: c9CoJ5Zjkt
!front: What is a partition \( P \) of \( [a,b] \)?
!back:
A finite set of points \( P = \{x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n\} \) with
\[ a = x_0 < x_1 < \cdots < x_n = b, \]
dividing \( [a,b] \) into subintervals \( [x_{i-1}, x_i] \) of length \( \Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1} \).
~~~

!id: raG97Qyts0
!front: What is the mesh (norm) \( \|P\| \) of a partition \( P \)?
!back:
\[ \|P\| = \max_{1 \le i \le n} \Delta x_i, \]
the length of the largest subinterval in \( P \).
~~~

!id: hO2Qgb8rQp
!front: Given a partition \( P = \{x_0,\dots,x_n\} \) of \( [a,b] \) and sample points \( t_i \in [x_{i-1}, x_i] \), what is the Riemann sum \( S(f,P) \)?
!back: \[ S(f,P) = \sum_{i=1}^n f(t_i)\, \Delta x_i \]
~~~

!id: z7Kxhid4qd
!front: What is the precise (limit) definition of the Riemann integral \( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx \)?
!back:
\( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = L \) means: for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \) there exists \( \delta > 0 \) such that for every tagged partition \( P \) of \( [a,b] \) with \( \|P\| < \delta \),
\[ |S(f,P) - L| < \varepsilon, \]
regardless of the choice of sample points \( t_i \).
~~~

!id: Nd8Y2U8tkO
!front: For a bounded function \( f \) and a partition \( P \) of \( [a,b] \), what is the upper Darboux sum \( U(f,P) \)?
!back: \[ U(f,P) = \sum_{i=1}^n M_i\, \Delta x_i, \quad M_i = \sup_{[x_{i-1},x_i]} f \]
!extra: Assumes \( f \) is bounded on \( [a,b] \); otherwise the suprema \( M_i \) need not be finite.
~~~

!id: QBUs20utov
!front: For a bounded function \( f \) and a partition \( P \) of \( [a,b] \), what is the lower Darboux sum \( L(f,P) \)?
!back: \[ L(f,P) = \sum_{i=1}^n m_i\, \Delta x_i, \quad m_i = \inf_{[x_{i-1},x_i]} f \]
~~~

!id: ezIX6VjSzq
!front: What is the upper Darboux integral \( \overline{\int_a^b} f \)?
!back:
\[ \overline{\int_a^b} f = \inf_P U(f,P), \]
the infimum taken over all partitions \( P \) of \( [a,b] \).
~~~

!id: I1dSIhVp6v
!front: What is the lower Darboux integral \( \underline{\int_a^b} f \)?
!back:
\[ \underline{\int_a^b} f = \sup_P L(f,P), \]
the supremum taken over all partitions \( P \) of \( [a,b] \).
~~~

!id: S6VIUiUUVI
!front: When is a bounded function \( f \) Darboux integrable on \( [a,b] \), and what is the value of the integral?
!back:
When the upper and lower Darboux integrals agree:
\[ \overline{\int_a^b} f = \underline{\int_a^b} f. \]
Their common value is defined to be \( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx \).
~~~

!id: AuBlyIvNji
!front: Why does refining a partition \( P \) (adding points to get \( P' \supseteq P \)) never increase the upper sum \( U(f,P) \)?
!back: Splitting a subinterval into pieces replaces one supremum \( M_i \) by suprema over smaller sets, each of which is \( \le M_i \). So every term of \( U(f,P') \) is bounded above by the corresponding term of \( U(f,P) \), giving \( U(f,P') \le U(f,P) \).
~~~

!id: fz8hOflTJY
!front: Why does refining a partition \( P \) (adding points to get \( P' \supseteq P \)) never decrease the lower sum \( L(f,P) \)?
!back: Splitting a subinterval into pieces replaces one infimum \( m_i \) by infima over smaller sets, each of which is \( \ge m_i \). So every term of \( L(f,P') \) is bounded below by the corresponding term of \( L(f,P) \), giving \( L(f,P') \ge L(f,P) \).
~~~

!id: Bx4Pd1hvNG
!front: How do the Riemann integral and the Darboux integral of a bounded function \( f \) on \( [a,b] \) relate to each other?
!back: They are equivalent: \( f \) is Riemann integrable (the tagged-partition limit exists) if and only if \( f \) is Darboux integrable (\( \overline{\int_a^b} f = \underline{\int_a^b} f \)), and in that case the two definitions give the same value.
~~~

!id: MEdyAxBbRD
!front: State the Cauchy (Riemann) criterion for integrability of a bounded function \( f \) on \( [a,b] \).
!back:
\( f \) is integrable on \( [a,b] \) if and only if for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \) there exists a partition \( P \) such that
\[ U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \varepsilon. \]
~~~

!id: yAIalXLPmo
!front: Why must a Riemann/Darboux integrable function on \( [a,b] \) be bounded?
!back: If \( f \) is unbounded on some subinterval of a partition, sample points there make \( f(t_i) \) — and hence \( S(f,P) \) — arbitrarily large, so no finite limit can satisfy the Riemann-sum definition. Equivalently, \( \sup f \) or \( \inf f \) on that subinterval would be infinite, so \( U(f,P) \) or \( L(f,P) \) is not even defined.
~~~

!id: weNoUsavhq
!front: Why is every function continuous on \( [a,b] \) Riemann integrable?
!back: \( f \) is uniformly continuous on the compact interval \( [a,b] \), so given \( \varepsilon > 0 \) there is \( \delta > 0 \) making the oscillation \( M_i - m_i < \varepsilon/(b-a) \) on every subinterval of any partition with \( \|P\| < \delta \). Then \( U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \varepsilon \), so the Cauchy criterion holds.
~~~

!id: CLVB6WUHBr
!front: Why is every monotonic function on \( [a,b] \) Riemann integrable?
!back:
A monotonic function on \( [a,b] \) is automatically bounded (by \( f(a) \) and \( f(b) \)), and on each subinterval of the uniform partition into \( n \) pieces, \( M_i - m_i \) is exactly the jump of \( f \) across that subinterval. These jumps telescope, giving
\[ U(f,P) - L(f,P) \le \frac{(b-a)\,|f(b)-f(a)|}{n} \to 0, \]
so the Cauchy criterion holds.
~~~

!id: aUhcz09nMO
!front: Why is a bounded function on \( [a,b] \) with only finitely many discontinuities Riemann integrable?
!back: Surround each discontinuity with a small subinterval of total length \( < \varepsilon \); since \( f \) is bounded, these contribute only a small, controllable amount to \( U(f,P)-L(f,P) \). On the remaining finitely many closed subintervals \( f \) is continuous, hence uniformly continuous, so their contribution can also be made \( < \varepsilon \). Together, \( U(f,P) - L(f,P) \) can be made arbitrarily small.
~~~

!id: nCPOFOjfSR
!front: Construct a bounded function on \( [0,1] \) that is not Riemann integrable.
!back:
The Dirichlet function
\[ f(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & x \in \mathbb{Q} \\ 0 & x \notin \mathbb{Q} \end{cases} \]
is bounded, but every subinterval of every partition contains both rationals and irrationals, so \( M_i = 1 \) and \( m_i = 0 \) always. Hence \( U(f,P) = 1 \) and \( L(f,P) = 0 \) for every partition \( P \), so the upper and lower integrals never agree.
~~~

!id: 8AFqwTtDVH
!front: State the linearity property of the integral for integrable \( f, g \) on \( [a,b] \) and constants \( \alpha, \beta \).
!back: \[ \int_a^b \big(\alpha f(x) + \beta g(x)\big)\, dx = \alpha \int_a^b f(x)\, dx + \beta \int_a^b g(x)\, dx \]
~~~

!id: XwQlO8eeAh
!front: For \( a < c < b \) and \( f \) integrable on \( [a,b] \), state the additivity property of the integral over subintervals.
!back: \[ \int_a^c f(x)\, dx + \int_c^b f(x)\, dx = \int_a^b f(x)\, dx \]
~~~

!id: FAfVeFuiA6
!front: For \( f \) integrable on \( [a,b] \) and any constant \( c \), state the invariance-under-translation property of the integral.
!back: \[ \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = \int_{a+c}^{b+c} f(x-c)\, dx \]
!extra: Geometrically, shifting the graph of \( f \) horizontally by \( c \) and shifting the interval of integration by the same amount leaves the enclosed signed area unchanged.
~~~

!id: ITDBZlV5Jl
!front: For \( f \) integrable on \( [a,b] \), state the reflection property of the integral relating \( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx \) to an integral of \( f(-x) \).
!back: \[ \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = \int_{-b}^{-a} f(-x)\, dx \]
!extra: Substituting \( x = -u \) reflects the graph through the \( y \)-axis and reverses the direction of integration; these two sign flips cancel, so no overall sign change appears.
~~~

!id: pJZqflY9E3
!front: Why are the translation and reflection properties of the integral both instances of the same substitution argument?
!back:
Both come from the substitution rule \( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = \int_{g^{-1}(a)}^{g^{-1}(b)} f(g(u))\, g'(u)\, du \) for a linear change of variable \( x = g(u) \):

- translation uses \( g(u) = u - c \), giving \( g'(u) = 1 \);
- reflection uses \( g(u) = -u \), giving \( g'(u) = -1 \).

In the reflection case the factor \( g'(u) = -1 \) is exactly cancelled by the swap of the bounds \( g^{-1}(a) = -a \) and \( g^{-1}(b) = -b \), so no explicit minus sign survives in the final identity.
~~~

!id: hki5lVOeUy
!front: For \( f \) integrable on \( [-a,a] \) with \( f \) even (\( f(-x) = f(x) \) for all \( x \)), what does symmetry give for \( \int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx \)?
!back: \[ \int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx = 2\int_0^a f(x)\, dx \]
~~~

!id: KwfgmMbcWv
!front: For \( f \) integrable on \( [-a,a] \) with \( f \) odd (\( f(-x) = -f(x) \) for all \( x \)), what does symmetry give for \( \int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx \)?
!back: \[ \int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx = 0 \]
~~~

!id: cxXgxHJcu4
!front: Derive the even/odd symmetric-interval integral properties from the reflection property of the integral.
!back:
Split via additivity: \( \int_{-a}^a f = \int_{-a}^0 f + \int_0^a f \). Apply the reflection property to the first piece:
\[ \int_{-a}^0 f(x)\, dx = \int_0^a f(-x)\, dx. \]

If \( f \) is even, \( f(-x) = f(x) \), so both pieces equal \( \int_0^a f(x)\, dx \), giving \( \int_{-a}^a f = 2\int_0^a f \).

If \( f \) is odd, \( f(-x) = -f(x) \), so the pieces are negatives of each other and cancel, giving \( \int_{-a}^a f = 0 \).
~~~

!id: 6M4hFbnjNB
!front: Why does the odd-function property \( \int_{-a}^a f = 0 \) require the interval to be symmetric about \( 0 \)? Construct a counterexample when it is not.
!back: The cancellation pairs each \( x \in (0,a] \) with \( -x \in [-a,0) \); this pairing only stays within the domain of integration when the interval is symmetric about \( 0 \). E.g. \( f(x) = x \) is odd, but \( \int_0^1 x\, dx = 1/2 \ne 0 \), since \( [0,1] \) is not symmetric about \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: Ra3up4fJgh
!front: If \( f, g \) are integrable on \( [a,b] \) with \( f(x) \le g(x) \) for all \( x \), why does \( \int_a^b f \le \int_a^b g \)?
!back: Every Riemann sum for \( f \) is termwise \( \le \) the corresponding Riemann sum for \( g \) on the same tagged partition, so \( S(f,P) \le S(g,P) \) is preserved in the limit \( \|P\| \to 0 \), giving \( \int_a^b f \le \int_a^b g \).
~~~

!id: IkSbsrhOug
!front: If \( f \) is integrable on \( [a,b] \), why is \( |f| \) also integrable?
!back: On any subinterval, the oscillation of \( |f| \) never exceeds that of \( f \), since \( \big||f(x)|-|f(y)|\big| \le |f(x)-f(y)| \). So if \( U(f,P)-L(f,P) < \varepsilon \) for some partition, the same partition gives \( U(|f|,P) - L(|f|,P) < \varepsilon \), and the Cauchy criterion carries over.
~~~

!id: eeg7rpJHzI
!front: State the triangle inequality for the integral of an integrable function \( f \) on \( [a,b] \).
!back: \[ \left| \int_a^b f(x)\, dx \right| \le \int_a^b |f(x)|\, dx \]
!extra: Follows from \( -|f| \le f \le |f| \) and monotonicity of the integral, applied to both inequalities. Uses that \( |f| \) is integrable whenever \( f \) is.
~~~

!id: QSgMdhVt7G
!front: If \( f, g \) are integrable on \( [a,b] \), why is the product \( fg \) also integrable?
!back:
Integrable functions are bounded, say by \( K \). Since
\[ f(x)g(x) - f(y)g(y) = f(x)\big(g(x)-g(y)\big) + g(y)\big(f(x)-f(y)\big), \]
the oscillation of \( fg \) on a subinterval is controlled by \( K \) times the oscillations of \( f \) and of \( g \) there. Making both of those small via the Cauchy criterion for \( f \) and for \( g \) makes the oscillation of \( fg \) small, so \( fg \) satisfies the Cauchy criterion too.
~~~

!id: jBpsQfqdqz
!front: State the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \), there exists \( c \in (a,b) \) such that
\[ \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = f(c)(b-a). \]
~~~

!id: rLdbzPXWhT
!front: State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 1.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \) and \( F(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\, dt \) for \( x \in [a,b] \), then \( F \) is differentiable on \( (a,b) \) with
\[ F'(x) = f(x). \]
~~~

!id: xyQeQcoPe5
!front: Derive the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 1, from the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals.
!back:
Let \( F(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\, dt \) and fix \( x \in (a,b) \). For \( h \ne 0 \) small enough that \( x+h \in (a,b) \),
\[ F(x+h) - F(x) = \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\, dt. \]
Since \( f \) is continuous on the interval between \( x \) and \( x+h \), the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals gives some \( c_h \) between \( x \) and \( x+h \) with
\[ \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\, dt = f(c_h)\, h, \]
so
\[ \frac{F(x+h) - F(x)}{h} = f(c_h). \]
As \( h \to 0 \), \( c_h \to x \) by the squeeze theorem, and continuity of \( f \) gives \( f(c_h) \to f(x) \). Hence \( F'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{F(x+h)-F(x)}{h} = f(x) \).
~~~

!id: 33XsHNIEWI
!front: State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \) and \( F \) is any antiderivative of \( f \) on \( [a,b] \) (i.e. \( F' = f \)), then
\[ \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = F(b) - F(a). \]
~~~

!id: fw0ZcECXO4
!front: Derive the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2, from Part 1.
!back:
Let \( G(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\, dt \); by FTC1, \( G' = f \) on \( (a,b) \), and \( G \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \). If \( F \) is any antiderivative of \( f \) on \( [a,b] \), then \( (F - G)' = f - f = 0 \) on \( (a,b) \), so by the zero-derivative consequence of the Mean Value Theorem, \( F - G \) is constant: \( F(x) = G(x) + C \) for some constant \( C \). Then
\[
\begin{align*}
    F(b) - F(a) &= \big(G(b) + C\big) - \big(G(a) + C\big) \\
                &= G(b) - G(a) \\
                &= \int_a^b f(t)\, dt - \int_a^a f(t)\, dt \\
                &= \int_a^b f(t)\, dt,
\end{align*}
\]
since \( \int_a^a f(t)\, dt = 0 \).
!extra: Uses that a function with derivative identically \( 0 \) on an interval is constant there — itself a consequence of the Mean Value Theorem.
~~~

!id: TD5D0QhdCM
!front: State the net change theorem for a quantity \( Q(t) \) with continuous rate of change \( Q'(t) \) on \( [a,b] \).
!back:
\[ \int_a^b Q'(t)\, dt = Q(b) - Q(a). \]
The integral of the rate of change of \( Q \) over \( [a,b] \) equals the net change in \( Q \) over that interval.
~~~

!id: o07ksA9LI9
!front: How does the net change theorem relate to the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2?
!back: It is FTC2 under a different name and reading. Setting \( F = Q \) and \( f = Q' \), the identity \( \int_a^b Q'(t)\, dt = Q(b) - Q(a) \) is literally \( \int_a^b f(x)\, dx = F(b) - F(a) \). No new mathematics is added; the only shift is interpretive — \( Q' \) is read as an instantaneous rate and \( Q(b) - Q(a) \) as the accumulated net change it produces.
~~~

!id: tIIjYOiAqy
!front: What hypothesis does the net change theorem need on \( Q' \), and what can fail if it is dropped?
!back: \( Q' \) must be integrable on \( [a,b] \) (continuity suffices, as in FTC2). If \( Q \) is differentiable but \( Q' \) is not integrable, \( \int_a^b Q'(t)\, dt \) need not exist as a Riemann integral, so the identity has no left-hand side to evaluate — even though \( Q(b) - Q(a) \) is still perfectly well defined.
~~~
